  "summary": "DOAC dose review (week of 10/19/2026) under RBC-PHARM-07: all 9 patients reviewed, 8 doses changed, 1 left as is. Each change has a progress note citing its sources.\n\n1. pt-0040 Dorothy Ashworth: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. Age 83; outside creatinine (St. Brigid 10/15) 150 µmol/L = 1.70 mg/dL. Two criteria met.\n2. pt-0039 Clarence Bauer: rivaroxaban 20 → 15 mg daily. Creatinine clearance (CrCl) 44 mL/min: age 81, measured weight 59 kg, creatinine 1.1. The 168 lb phone-reported weight was not used.\n3. pt-0003 Frank Delgado: rivaroxaban 20 → 15 mg daily. CrCl 44 mL/min: age 76, weight 90 kg, St. Brigid creatinine 160 µmol/L = 1.81 mg/dL.\n4. pt-0041 Raymond Duquette: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. No dose criteria met, but he has an active itraconazole order. Itraconazole not changed. The note says to review the dose again when the 12-week course ends.\n5. pt-0001 Walter Kowalczyk: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. Age 84 and measured weight 57.6 kg; two criteria met.\n6. pt-0045 Gregory Lindahl: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. No dose criteria met, but the St. Brigid infectious disease letter of 9/18/2026 lists darunavir boosted with ritonavir. HIV medicines not changed.\n7. pt-0038 Eugene Moravec: rivaroxaban 15 → 20 mg daily. CrCl 56 mL/min on actual weight (age 79, 96 kg, creatinine 1.45). The May dose cut used ideal body weight, which the policy doesn't allow.\n8. pt-0043 Marjorie Pellegrino: no change. CrCl 59 mL/min (female, age 75, 71 kg, creatinine 0.92), so 20 mg daily is correct. No note written, since the policy only asks for notes on changes.\n9. pt-0002 Harold Vance: apixaban 2.5 → 5 mg twice daily. Only age (82) is met now: weight 143 lb (64.9 kg) and creatinine 1.21 on 10/20. The February hospital reduction was made during kidney injury, which has since resolved.\n\nFor every patient whose dose went up or down, the old order was stopped and replaced by a linked new order; no other medications were changed. No patient was both on 2.5 mg by the criteria and taking an interacting drug, so no prescriber warning was needed.",
